/P 100 0 R
/E (The Middle East has long stood as one of the most strategically pivotal and politically intricate regions on the globe. Positioned at the crossroads of Asia, Africa, and Europe, its geopolitical weight is anchored not only in vast energy reserves but also in its deep historical, religious, and political fractures. For decades, the region has been trapped in cycles of structural instability driven by unresolved territorial disputes, entrenched state-on-state rivalries, sharp ideological divides, and the complex interplay between sovereign governments and non-state armed groups. Because these local grievances are deeply interwoven across national borders, localized frictions rarely remain contained; a single tactical miscalculation or border flare-up possesses the inherent potential to cascade rapidly into a multi-theater crisis.)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
